From: openEuler RISC-V Maintainers <noreply@example.invalid>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 04:10:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Make reallocarray oversize assertion independent of RLIMIT_AS

QEMU linux-user deliberately ignores guest RLIMIT_AS updates to avoid
breaking its own address space. The upstream test assumes setrlimit limits a
later 1 GiB realloc, which can instead succeed under user-mode emulation.
Keep the entire test suite and all assertions, but request a non-overflowing
SIZE_MAX-1 bytes in the oversize case. Linux/glibc rejects that allocation
with ENOMEM even when QEMU ignores the resource limit. Production code is
unchanged.

Patch-Source: downstream QEMU user-mode compatibility
Patch-License: MIT
Upstream-Status: Not submitted (test-harness compatibility only)
Remove-When: The upstream test no longer relies on RLIMIT_AS under QEMU user mode.
---
 test/reallocarray.c | 2 +-
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/test/reallocarray.c b/test/reallocarray.c
--- a/test/reallocarray.c
+++ b/test/reallocarray.c
@@ -158,7 +158,7 @@ static void test_Xmureallocarray_oversize(void)
     CHECK_ALIGNMENT(p);
     CHECK_SIZE(p, num * size);

-    p2 = Xmureallocarray(p, 2, ALLOC_LIMIT);
+    p2 = Xmureallocarray(p, 2, SIZE_MAX / 2);
     g_assert_null(p2);
     /* Unfortunately, g_assert_null has a test_nonfatal_assertions option that
